Cardiac Arrest
Acute Urgent Care
Sudden loss of consciousness, Absent central pulse, Apnoea/agonal breathing, Shockable or non-shockable rhythm on ECG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-022
Reason for Visit: Sudden collapse, unresponsiveness, and cessation of normal breathing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: The adult child frantically reported that the patient had suddenly collapsed, turned blue, and stopped breathing normally. This constitutes a catastrophic medical emergency requiring immediate, on-the-spot intervention to restore circulation. Any delay or reliance on standard after-hours wait times equates to certain biological death. I happened to be in the immediate vicinity when the emergency call was routed.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Bystander report] He clutched his chest, fell to the floor, and now he won't wake up and is making gasping noises!
History of Present Illness: The bystander reports a sudden and complete loss of consciousness occurring approximately 4 minutes prior to my arrival. The patient had complained of severe chest pain moments before the collapse. Upon collapse, the patient exhibited total unresponsiveness to all verbal and painful stimuli. The bystander noted that the patient was not breathing normally, only demonstrating occasional, irregular gasps (agonal breathing).
Functional Status: Unconscious, pulseless, and apnoeic.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening event; bystander is in severe shock.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Unrecordable | HR: 0 | Temp: N/A | RR: 0 | SpO2: Unrecordable | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient is lying supine on the floor, profoundly pale with central cyanosis, and completely flaccid.
Physical Exam: Primary survey reveals an absolute absence of a palpable central pulse (carotid and femoral). The patient is apnoeic with only rare agonal gasping respirations. Pupils are dilated and unreactive to light. There is a complete loss of motor tone. Blood pressure is unmeasurable. The clinical picture is definitive for cardiac arrest. Expected investigation results would involve the application of a cardiac monitor/defibrillator revealing either ventricular fibrillation, pulseless ventricular tachycardia, pulseless electrical activity, or asystole.
Environmental Observations: Clear space on the living room floor; adequate room for CPR and paramedic equipment.
Medication Reconciliation: N/A in the acute resuscitation phase.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cardiac arrest - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absolute absence of a central pulse and the lack of spontaneous breathing, which confirm circulatory collapse rather than a transient drop in blood pressure.

Massive Seizure - Ruled out/unlikely due to the flaccid motor tone and absence of a pulse, whereas a seizing patient will typically have a palpable, often tachycardic pulse.

Massive Stroke/Intracranial Haemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary immediate cause of death, though it could precipitate an arrest; the immediate issue is the lack of cardiac output requiring mechanical resuscitation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with the definitive tetrad of cardiac arrest: sudden loss of consciousness, absent central pulse, and apnoea/agonal breathing, pending ECG rhythm confirmation. The situation represents the ultimate medical emergency. My immediate physical presence was required to initiate high-quality cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and coordinate the arrival of emergency medical services. Time to basic life support and defibrillation is the sole determinant of survival in this scenario; delaying intervention would have resulted in irreversible anoxic brain injury and death within minutes.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Verified 000 had been called by the bystander. Immediately commenced high-quality chest compressions at a rate of 100-120 per minute. Applied Automated External Defibrillator (AED) retrieved from my medical kit. AED indicated a "Shockable Rhythm" (suspected Ventricular Fibrillation). Delivered 1 shock. Resumed CPR immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change: By pushing hard and fast in the center of the chest the moment someone collapses and stops breathing normally, you manually pump oxygenated blood to their brain, keeping it alive until the defibrillator arrives.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Advanced Life Support (ALS) Paramedics in transit.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to ALS paramedics for continued resuscitation and transport.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Continue uninterrupted CPR and follow AED prompts until ALS paramedics arrive and take over the scene.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The patient's heart has completely stopped pumping. This is a critical situation requiring aggressive resuscitation to attempt to restart it.
Clinical Course: Survival depends entirely on the underlying cause of the cardiac arrest and the speed at which the heart can be shocked back into a normal rhythm.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure the front door is wide open for the paramedics. Stay clear of the patient when the defibrillator instructs to deliver a shock.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I am manually pumping his heart to force blood to his brain. The machine will analyze his heart's electrical system and deliver a shock to try and reset it.
Actionable Steps: Go stand by the front door and wave the paramedics inside the second they pull up. Do not interrupt my compressions.

